{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>You shall not leave until morning the sacrifice of the feast of the Pesach.</b> According to its plain sense, it warns concerning the entire festival that nothing of the flesh should remain until morning, for what is left of it until morning shall be burned in fire (Exodus 12:10), and concerning the fat, that it should not be offered beyond morning. And Onkelos (Onkelos on Exodus 34:25) translated it as referring to the fat that goes up on high, for it is the festival, as it says there (Exodus 23:18), \"the fat of my feast shall not remain until morning,\" for all these commandments are repetitions. And Rashi wrote (Rashi on Exodus 34:25): \"You shall not leave\" — according to its translation, overnighting does not apply to the top of the altar, but overnighting applies only at dawn — \"the sacrifice of the feast of the Pesach\" — its emurim (sacrificial portions), and from here you learn for all burning of fats and limbs. And in the portion of Tsav (Leviticus 6:2) I will explain this, God willing. Overnighting on the top of the altar is a dispute in the Gemara (Zevaḥim 87a), and according to Rava's opinion there is overnighting on the top of the altar, and if the limbs remained overnight there and descended, they are not brought back up, for if they did not descend, even if they remained overnight on the ground for many days and were then brought up, they would not be taken down, as is taught in the chapter The Altar (ibid.)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :register {:flags {0 ["God"]}}, :notes {0 "The Aramaic phrase עולה לגבוה ('goes up on high') is the standard sacrificial term; rendered literally to preserve Onkelos's usage as cited by Ramban."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:21:32.054967249Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}